  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 107 PM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe drying and warming trend continues for Sunday and Monday with \ntemperatures across the plains anticipated to crest over 100 once \nagain.  Heat Advisories have been issued for the urban areas of the \nI-25 corridor where temperatures in the upper 90s to lower 100s put \nit into the higher end of the Heat Impact Levels.  There could still \nbe some isolated thunderstorms across the southern mountains, but \nthese will be higher based with more of a gusty outflow wind \npotential, and only spotty moderate rainfall.  Monday looks similar \nbut a few degrees hotter and it is likely that more Heat Advisories \nwill be needed over a more widespread area of the southeast plains. \nWill let later shifts hone down that area as we get closer.  Highs \nup to 106 will be possible on Monday.  \n\nA cool front pushes into the southeast plains Monday night with low \nlevel flow shifting around from the east on Tuesday.  This will \nbring another uptick in thunderstorms for the southeast mountains \nand plains depending on how quickly low level moisture advects back \nwestward.  This will increase the risk of flash flooding on Aspen \nAcres.  \n\nAfter a cool down for Tuesday, temperatures gradually warm again, \nbut the area will be under the influence of northwest flow aloft \nwith embedded shortwaves bringing episodic thunderstorm chances to \nthe area most days.  Main monsoon tap looks suppressed to the south \nso moisture quality doesn't looks as high as it was this week. \nPrecipitable waters remain around to a little below normal through \nthe period.  So the signal for burn scar flash flooding looks \nminimal for the time being.  But temperatures will be rebounding \nback towards the century mark across the plains by late week.\n-KT",
